{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And they said.</b> We did not transgress your command, for the Hebrew women are not like the Egyptian women, for they have much vitality. And the yod is weak [i.e., without dagesh], from כי חיות — and this is correct, like דבת רות עלת עלי (Genesis 33:13). And the yod is strengthened [with dagesh] in the word חַיִּים, which is an adjective, like וּנְתָפְשָׂם חַיִּים (2 Kings 7:12). [This is] like the yod of אֱלֹהֵי הָעִבְרִיִּים (below, 3:18). So too חיים, which is a noun, like עֵץ חַיִּים (Proverbs 3:18), and like וּפְקֻעֵי צִצִּים (1 Kings 6:18)." "<b>וְיָלָדוּ.</b> Feminine plural, past tense verb, for it is identical to the masculine forms טָווּ (below, 35:25), צָבְאוּ (ibid., 38:8)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The word חיות appears in the biblical text as חיות (Exodus 1:19); Ibn Ezra's discussion of the weak vs. strengthened yod concerns the phonology of חיות vs. חיים. The verb forms he cites (דבת, רות, עלת) are cited in their biblical consonantal spelling with weak vav."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:30:56.261369749Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}